we start with breaking news russian news  agencies are reporting that mikhail  gorbachev the former soviet leader who  helped bring the cold war to a peaceful  conclusion  has died he was 91 years old with  policies that promoted free speech and  reform within the communist bloc  gorbachev forged a new dialogue with the  west it was one that ultimately brought  down the iron curtain that had divided  europe since 1945.  his policies also helped put germany on  the path to reunification  [Applause]  mikhail gorbachev arrived and freedom  followed  when he visited east berlin for the  gdr's 40th anniversary celebrations  gorbachev urged erichonica to implement  reforms  he warned the east german leader saying  life punishes those who come too late  [Music]  a month after his visit the berlin wall  fell  and with it the iron curtain that had  divided europe for decades  [Music]  it was gorbachev's reformers policies  that helped unleash a peaceful  revolution across eastern europe  he laid out his agenda in his first  speech as leader of the soviet union  a stark contrast to what had gone before  the right to live in peace and freedom  is the most important basic right of  each individual  [Applause]  gorbachev wanted to change the soviet  union  to bring it closer to the people  he promoted glasnost openness and  perestroika reforms  two words that will forever be  associated with him  his new outlook was popular with many  his policies also applied to the  military despite the resistance of  soviet generals  i'm convinced that we will also have to  reform our military  we have far too many weapons  we have to seek dialogue with other  superpowers and reduce our arsenals to a  reasonable extent  [Applause]  as a result of that dialogue gorbachev  and u.s president reagan signed the  first of many disarmament treaties in  1987.  [Applause]  it was the beginning of the end of the  cold war  in 1990 gorbachev was awarded the nobel  peace prize for his key role in bringing  about a new peaceful world order  internationally he was highly regarded  but at home he was increasingly under  pressure  the economic situation was dire the  soviet union on the verge of falling  apart  communist hardliners staged a coup  against gorbachev in 1991 and put the  soviet leader under house arrest in his  holiday retreat in crimea  one man stopped them boris yeltsin the  russian president  [Applause]  gorbachev was allowed to return to  moscow  but politically he was ruined  yeltsin banned the communist party and  publicly humiliated gorbachev  shortly afterwards the soviet union was  dissolved  in december 1991 mikhail gorbachev  announced his resignation  his attempts to regain political ground  in the new russia failed  many russians held him responsible for  the demise of the soviet union but while  his own country turned its back on him  gorbachev was hailed around the world as  the man who helped tear down the iron  curtain  and bring about a peaceful  revolution we want to pull in our  correspondent roman goncharenko for more  roman we want to let our viewers know  you met mikhail gorbachev personally i  want to talk about that in just a moment  but first i just want to get your  reaction um like everyone around the  world we're just hearing about the  passing of mikhail gorbachev what did  you think when you heard the news  well um as many i've been expecting that  because there were reports that he was  very ill very sick and he is 92 years  old um when we heard that so um  he was an old man and um  it is no surprise but still it's it's um  this this news is is has struck me i  have to confess because uh he's such a  huge historical figure  um  historical figure not just for russia  but all the former soviet republics  including um ukraine where the war is  now going on  and um of course for the west for the  whole world so his legacy is extremely  important it is actually being destroyed  by president putin at this very moment  during this war in ukraine i've just  referred to  there when we talk about collective  memory of mikhail gorbachev there is the  memory that we have of him  here in the west  and there's also the memory that has  been pushed inside russia and i dare say  that those are two very different  notions about mikhail gorbachev aren't  they  of course but things change you know um  gorbachev was a hero for the soviet  people in the late 90s and early um  late 80s and early 90s  so he was the one who gave people  freedom who gave freedom of speech the  words glassnest and perestroika  meaning that change is coming possible  we can the soviet union or  later russia another republic could  leave in peace with the west that's that  was the key message he gave people the  right to travel actually  because the soviet union was a closed  empire so you couldn't just go abroad  before him  and uh millions of people are grateful  for that  um and uh  later in the 90s and and when putin came  to power in russia um the attitude  changed  most of most people disliked gorbachev  uh they blame him uh for the collapse of  the soviet union  um  they think that he was too soft with the  west that the west betrayed him  this is what most russians think today  for a minority in russia he's still a  hero  and  they are still very grateful to him  for for giving the russians and other  peoples of the former soviet union just  what i've just said freedom first of all  the right to travel freedom of speech um  democracy so um um i do hope  that maybe in 10 or 20 years we don't  know um how russia is developing  but i think that good and and um towards  gorbachev could change as well and come  back to the roots where it all started  he is a historic figure  and how can you imagine how  russia is going to  mark his passing i mean he was leader of  the soviet union  normally we would expect there to be  some type of state funeral for someone  like mikhail gorbachev is that something  that um you expect  in a russia that is being led by  vladimir putin  it is difficult to imagine a big  [Music]  state funeral because he is very much  disliked by by the majority of russians  but  you never know  there was  when his uh when when boris yeltsin the  successor of gorbachev was president of  russia and the first president of russia  died  there was kind of a state funeral but it  was not uh so so big and huge i would  say so we could expect something like  that uh now in this case uh we do not  know how russian propaganda will try to  use it but we have to remember this war  in ukraine i've mentioned at the  beginning of our conversation is  actually about dismantling what  gorbachev created it's about turning  back the clock  and trying to restore the soviet union  by force  something which uh or all expert experts  i've talked to and and uh people i've  talked to  are very happy didn't happen in the  early 90s when when the soviet union  collapsed  peacefully  and uh 30 years later we are relieving a  kind of that collapse with with a lot of  blood and dying and this is this is very  tragic actually actually  roman talk to me before we run out of  time talk to me about when you met  mikhail gorbachev  well he was  it was i was very lucky to meet him in  moscow in 2009 we were talking about  the  fall of the burning wall in germany and  why he didn't use force at that time  and i asked him  if he was  [Music]  pushed by maybe some military to use  force and he said no i was very popular  and um  people supported me not to use force in  eastern germany and i think this is also  something that germany will remember him  for for being for for for letting the  urinification happen for letting the  burning wall fall  and uh this is his historic achievement  and he will be remembered for that  dw correspondent roman goncharenko on  the passing of former soviet leader  mikhail gorbachev roman thank you
 

